ABOUT THE POSTER

The convention is that the latest poster PDF is "umsposter20XX.pdf" in
this directory, replacing XX with the year.


ABOUT THE IMAGES IN THIS DIRECTORY

The poster needs to be converted to two PNG images (truecolor RGB, 8 bpp).

1) umsposter20XX-big.png
FULL PAGE IMAGE, no cropping or border
Image resolution should be 656x1031
= 62.5dpi if the source PDF is 10.5 x 16.5 inches

HOW TO MAKE THIS FILE:

* Conversion using ImageMagick, which works well if the poster is made
with inkscape and has no color space data:

  convert -density 62.5 umsposter20XX.pdf umsposter20XX-big.png

If there are aliasing problems (jagged edges), it may be better to first
make a version at 250dpi and then scale down to 25%.

or

* Conversion using 'pdftoppm', which works well if the poster is made in
Adobe Illustrator (where color space data tends to cause problems with
ImageMagick):

  pdftoppm umsposter20XX.pdf umsposter20XX-big -png -rx 62.5 -ry 62.5


2) umsposter20XX-thumb.png
CROPPED image, removing blue top and bottom border
Image resolution 144x193
Before cropping the PDF is converted to a 144x226 image (13.7dpi at
10.5 x 16.5 inches)

Then we crop to a 142x191 rectangle centered at coordinates (1,17)
(Equivalently, we remove a strip of 17 pixels from the top, 18 pixels
from the bottom, and then one column of pixels from each side)
A 1-pixel black border is then added, bringing the size to 


HOW TO MAKE THIS FILE:

* Conversion using ImageMagick (generally good for Inkscape-produced PDF):

  convert -density 13.7 umsposter20XX.pdf -crop 142x191+1+17 -bordercolor Black -border 1 umsposter20XX-thumb.png

* Conversion using 'pdftoppm' (generally good for Adobe Illustrator PDF):

  pdftoppm umsposter20XX.pdf umsposter20XX-thumb-precrop -png -rx 13.7 -ry 13.7
  convert -crop 142x191+1+17 -bordercolor Black -border 1 umsposter20XX-thumb-precrop-1.png umsposter20XX-thumb.png

* Sometimes it is necessary to use a real image editor like GIMP to make
small corrections (e.g. if text extends slightly into the intended
border bands on the top and bottom, as happened in 2018).


ABOUT index.html

It contains hard-coded filenames (by year) so it needs to be updated.

The main point of this index.html is so that one can share

  http://math.uic.edu/ums/poster/

on social media and this will force the thumbnail to be of the poster.
This page displays a centered image of the poster which is a clickable
link to the UMS homepage.